 her thinking that he had  contacts inside mi5 he was very  plausible  he arranged meetings in underground car  parks  scary places at the best of times but  when you think that your life is in  danger it was  it was very potent so she was  she was put in a state of fear and  trepidation um  and that encouraged her to speak out and  she  and so when she gave the interview it  wasn't a question of self-indulgence as  many people thought  but it was a question of self  preservation she thought that she was a  target  she thought that what from what bashir  said she was  she was um under the gun  or in the in the sights of the  establishment and remember  she lived like this for most of her  adult life  so in a way you're lining up with the  the duke of cambridge here because  prince william  said last week it is my view that the  deceitful way the interview was obtained  substantially influenced what my mother  said the interview was a major  contribution to making my parents  relationship  worse and has since hurt countless  others  and his brother said the ripple effect  of a culture of exploitation  and unethical practices ultimately took  her life i mean in a sense  what all three of you appear to be  saying is  that the manner in which martin bashir  got this provoked a kind of  i think the words has been used as  paranoia in the princess  that meant that what she then said uh  was  reflected that fear  now i i i disagree with william and  harry on this  and and i do find it ironic that it's a  bunch of  old etonians uh including earl spencer  who think there's a line between  the interview and diana's death i mean  diana wanted to speak her truth  and and she'd been stifled for a long  time  so yes she was she was terrified into  doing the interview by bashir  but no well what she had to say was  very much reflected what you really  truly felt three of us in the marriage  queen of people's hearts all these  things that she would have probably said  these kinds of things to somebody else  uh nicholas whitchloss  some other uh journalist it so happened  that bashir  uh beat the pack by lying to her and  that's how he did it  where do you think this leaves relations  between the royal family and the bbc  well relations between the bbc and the  royal family were  at an all-time low after the panorama  interview they  they uh really turned their backs on  them and they've been rebuilt over the  years  now of course when you've got these  extraordinary public statements given by  uh prince william and prince harry uh  attacking the bbc then they've got a  long way to go to restore that trust  you're a distinguished journalist andrew  um  how does what's been revealed by  this scandal leave you feeling  about our trade  well this is  this is the phone tapping moment for the  bbc and remember  the news of the world was involved in  tapping  uh harry and william's phones and that  that newspaper was shut down  this this is a a black day for for  british journalism  and and i think that we should all  reflect on  how you obtain an interview and the  process by which  you carry it forwards the bbc's  phone tapping moment thank you very much  andrew morton
 

